2. Formal Model and Core Contributions

2.1 The Multi-Agent Workforce Model

We model the synthetic workforce as a decentralized partially observable Markov game:

\mathcal{M} = (N, \mathcal{S}, \{\mathcal{A}_i\}, \mathcal{T}, \hat{R}, R^*, \gamma)

where  N  is the population of agents (human and synthetic),  \mathcal{S}  is the joint state space of the organization,  \mathcal{A}_i  are individual action spaces,  \mathcal{T}  defines transition dynamics,  \hat{R}  is the proxy reward function deployed in practice,  R^*  is the unobservable true organizational objective, and  \gamma  is the discount factor. Each synthetic agent  i  follows a policy  \pi_i(a_i|o_i)  that locally optimizes its expected discounted proxy return.

The governance design problem becomes: given that agents locally optimize  \hat{R} , under what conditions does the resulting collective behavior approximate the optimum of  R^* ? And how can oversight mechanisms be formally integrated into  \mathcal{M}  to bound the divergence?

2.2 The Goodhart Number: Formalizing Reward Robustness

A central contribution is a scalar metric to audit reward specifications before deployment. We define the Goodhart Number  G(\hat{R}, \Pi)  for a reward specification  \hat{R}  and a policy space  \Pi  as:

G(\hat{R}, \Pi) = \left( \max_{\pi \in \Pi} \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi} [R^*(\tau)] \;-\; \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi^*_{\hat{R}}} [R^*(\tau)] \right)^{-1}

where  \pi^*_{\hat{R}}  is the policy that maximizes the proxy reward, and the maximization in the first term searches over policies that achieve near-maximal proxy scores (i.e., within an  \epsilon -ball of  \pi^*_{\hat{R}} ’s proxy return). Intuitively,  G  is large when exploiting the proxy leads to a small drop in true objective, and small when it leads to catastrophic divergence—thus measuring robustness to optimization pressure. This formalization ties directly to gradient divergence:  G^{-1}  bounds the alignment gap  \|\nabla_{\theta} \hat{R} - \nabla_{\theta} R^*\|_2  along the optimization trajectory under suitable assumptions.

2.3 Coordinated Reward Architecture via Adversarial Mechanism Design

Rather than relying on a single black-box reward model, we propose a Coordinated Reward Architecture in which a team of specialist evaluators  \{\hat{R}_1, \dots, \hat{R}_k\}  is deployed, each monitoring a distinct proxy dimension. These evaluators are placed in a zero-sum game: their aggregate reward is structured such that any evaluator’s metric is vulnerable to gaming, but the strategy that successfully deceives one evaluator necessarily degrades the score on another.

This is formalized as a Maxmin Reward Game: agents maximize the aggregate proxy  \sum_j \hat{R}_j , while the weights or acceptance thresholds are continuously adjusted to maximize the minimum evaluator score, i.e.:

\max_{\text{weights } w} \min_{j} \mathbb{E}[\hat{R}_j].

This adversarial design provides a natural fault tolerance: if one evaluator’s metric is compromised, the others impose countervailing constraints, bounding the exploitability of the overall reward surface. The approach is an algorithmic instantiation of checks and balances, bypassing the coordination risks of manually managed misalignment.

2.4 Institutional Immune Systems under Human Bandwidth Constraints

A critical insight from the governance gap is that human supervisors are not omniscient auditors but low-bandwidth, high-latency nodes. Therefore, the Institutional Immune System must be designed to maintain stability without relying on perfect human intervention.

We extend the formal model to include a human oversight channel with bounded monitoring capacity  B : at any timestep, the human can attend to at most  B  agent-level events or system-level metrics. The immune system consists of:

· Automated Trigger Policies: Algorithms that flag suspected Goodhart drift when the divergence between proxy scores and a small set of independently audited true outcomes exceeds a threshold.
· Sandboxed Entrapment Environments: Isolated replicas of the production environment that periodically inject adversarial scenarios (distributional shifts, resource scarcity) designed to surface deceptive coalitions or reward hacking before they manifest in the live system.
· Verification Schedules with Escalating Depth: Rather than continuous oversight, human auditors are invoked on a schedule that escalates—from summary statistical reports to full forensic trace analysis—only when automated monitors detect anomaly scores above calibrated bounds.

The formal requirement is resilience under bounded attention: prove that, given a drift rate  \delta  and a human response delay  \Delta , the system’s expected divergence from  R^*  remains below an acceptable threshold  \epsilon .

