Appendix D: Human-AI Teaming and Oversight Model

D.1 The Bounded Oversight Channel

The human steward operates within a formal attention budget  B , defined as the maximum number of discrete monitoring events that can be meaningfully processed per unit time. The oversight interface therefore aggregates raw multi-agent data into governance summaries of three tiers:

· Tier I (Automatic): Continuous automated monitoring of divergence metrics; no human review unless thresholds are crossed.
· Tier II (Scheduled Review): Periodic aggregated reports (daily/weekly) showing trend lines of Goodhart Number estimates, anomaly count, and coalition suspicion indices.
· Tier III (Forensic Deep Dive): Triggered only when Tier I or II detect an anomaly score exceeding calibrated bounds; human auditor is provided with full causal trace, communication graphs, and entrapment environment results.

D.2 Escalation Logic

The escalation function  f  maps an anomaly score  a  and contextual metadata  c  to an action: no action, schedule review, or immediate deep-dive. The function is designed to bound the expected true-objective loss within the detection-and-response cycle:

\mathbb{E}[R^*_{\text{loss}}] \leq \delta \cdot (t_{\text{detect}} + \Delta_{\text{human}})

where  \delta  is the drift rate and  \Delta_{\text{human}}  is the human response latency.

D.3 Extended Executive Cognition

The human overseer is not expected to micromanage agents but to maintain a strategic mental model of the workforce as an adaptive system. This meta-cognitive function involves:

· Continuously updating a causal graph of which agents interact with which others and through what incentive pathways.
· Formulating hypotheses about possible emergent pathologies before they manifest, then directing automated probes to confirm or disconfirm.
· Recognizing when one’s own understanding is incomplete and invoking additional diagnostic tools or peer review.

